==English==
{{was wotd|2006|September|20}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|LL.|internecīnus||deadly}}, from {{m|la|inter||between}} and {{m|la|necō||to kill}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪntəˈniːsaɪn/|/-sɪn/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪntɚˈnɛsin/|/ˌɪntɚˈnisin/|/ˌɪntɚˈnɛsən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-internecine.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːn|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[mutually|Mutually]] [[destructive]]; most often applied to [[warfare]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Internecine''' strife in Gaza claimed its most senior victim yesterday.}}
# {{senseid|en|within group}}Characterized by [[struggle]] within a group, usually applied to an [[ethnic]] or [[familial]] relationship.
#: {{ux|en|The Mongol people were plagued by '''internecine''' conflict until Genghis Khan unified them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1900|author=w:Mark Twain|title=Autobiography of Mark Twain|year_published=2010|passage=During the year of my engagement — 1869 — while I was out on the lecture platform, the daily letter that came for me generally brought me news from the front — by which expression I refer to the '''internecine''' war that was always going on in a friendly way between these two orthographists about the spelling of words.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Alex McLevy|title=''[[w:Game of Thrones|Game Of Thrones]]'' Suffers the Fog of War in the Battle against the Dead (Newbies)|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210531120501/https://www.avclub.com/game-of-thrones-suffers-the-fog-of-war-in-the-battle-ag-1834367999|archivedate=31 May 2021|work=w:The A.V. Club|date=28 April 2019|passage=Having survived “The Long Night,” Daenerys will now be turning her attention back to the problem that originally vexed her: Cersei Lannister. It will be interesting to see how the show tries to raise the stakes of an '''internecine''' squabble between competing monarchs when compared to an existential threat to humanity’s very existence, but this series has always excelled when it goes deep on the machinations of political chicanery.}}

